g('how-to-export-from-india-step-by-step', 'export', 'How to export from India|step by step',
  'How to export goods from India: IEC, RCMC, HS code, buyer contract, packing, shipping bill, let export order, bill of lading and getting paid.',
  'Exporting is a chain of small steps. Miss one and the cargo misses the vessel. This is the order that works.',
  [('Set up once', ['Get an IEC from DGFT.', 'Open a bank account that handles foreign exchange and note the authorised dealer code for shipping bills.', 'Join the export promotion council for your product to get an RCMC, which many benefits need.', 'Register for GST and file a Letter of Undertaking (LUT) so you can export without paying IGST.']),
   ('For every order', ['Confirm the HS code, the Incoterm, the payment terms and any buyer rules or certificates.', 'Check that the product is not restricted for export and that you have any licence or NOC needed.', 'Pack and mark the cargo for the mode and the destination. Wood packing needs the ISPM 15 mark.', 'Book the container or air space with your forwarder.']),
   ('Customs and shipping', ['Prepare the commercial invoice and packing list. Your broker files the shipping bill on ICEGATE.', 'Move the cargo to the port, ICD or airport. Customs examines or passes it and gives a let export order.', 'The carrier loads the cargo, files the manifest and issues the bill of lading or airway bill.']),
   ('After shipment', ['Send the documents to the buyer or the bank as the payment terms require.', 'Get paid through your bank and keep the proof. The bank issues an e-BRC for the shipment, which you need for GST refunds and incentives.', 'Claim drawback, RoDTEP and any GST refund that applies.'])],
  [('Is an RCMC compulsory?', 'Not to export, but you need it to claim many policy benefits.'),
   ('Can I export without a customs broker?', 'You can file your own shipping bill, but most exporters use a licensed broker for speed and accuracy.'),
   ('When do I get the bill of lading?', 'After the cargo is loaded and the carrier has the let export order. Plan for a day or more after sailing for the final BL.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('shipping-bill-explained', 'export', 'Shipping bill|types, filing and what it does',
  'What a shipping bill is, the types (free, dutiable, drawback), how it is filed on ICEGATE and how it leads to the let export order.',
  'The shipping bill is the main customs document for an export from India. It tells customs what is leaving and, in many cases, it is also your claim for export benefits.',
  [('What goes in it', 'Exporter and IEC, buyer, HS code, description, quantity, value, destination, port of loading, Incoterm and the details of any export benefit you claim, such as drawback or RoDTEP.'),
   ('Types', [('table', ['Type', 'Used for'], [['Free shipping bill', 'Goods that carry no export duty and no drawback claim'], ['Dutiable shipping bill', 'Goods that carry an export duty'], ['Drawback shipping bill', 'Goods where a duty drawback is claimed'], ['Duty exempt or under scheme', 'Exports under schemes like advance authorisation']])]),
   ('Filing and the let export order', 'Your broker files the shipping bill on ICEGATE. Goods are handed over at the customs area, may be examined, and customs gives the let export order. The shipping bill is then the basis for the export manifest and your benefits.'),
   ('Why accuracy matters', 'The shipping bill data feeds drawback, RoDTEP and GST refund claims. A wrong HS code, value or bank detail can hold up the money for months.')],
  [('Who files it?', 'The exporter or the customs broker acting for the exporter.'),
   ('Can I amend a shipping bill?', 'Yes, in many cases, before or after the let export order, with approval and sometimes a fee.'),
   ('Is it the same as the bill of lading?', 'No. The shipping bill goes to customs. The bill of lading is the carrier receipt and title document.')],
  ('icegate-status-guide', 'Open the ICEGATE status guide'))
g('gst-lut-export-letter-of-undertaking', 'export', 'GST LUT for exporters|export without paying IGST',
  'What a GST Letter of Undertaking (LUT) is, who can use it, how to file it, and how it changes IGST and refunds on exports.',
  'Exports are zero rated under GST. You can either pay IGST and claim a refund, or file a Letter of Undertaking and export without paying it.',
  [('The two options', [('table', ['Route', 'What happens'], [['Export under LUT', 'No IGST paid on the export. You claim a refund of unused input tax credit.'], ['Export with payment of IGST', 'You pay IGST on the export and claim it back as a refund.']])]),
   ('Why most exporters choose LUT', 'It keeps cash free. You do not fund the tax and wait for the refund.'),
   ('How to file', 'File the LUT online on the GST portal, once for each financial year, before the first export. A good LUT stays valid for that year. File a new one every year.'),
   ('Keep in mind', ['Use the LUT number on the invoice and the shipping bill.', 'If you do not get the foreign payment inside the time allowed, GST rules can require you to pay the tax with interest.', 'Refund claims depend on correct shipping bill and e-BRC data.'])],
  [('Who can file a LUT?', 'Registered exporters of goods or services, subject to the conditions in the GST rules.'),
   ('Is a LUT needed for every shipment?', 'No. One LUT covers the financial year.'),
   ('What if I forget to renew it?', 'Exports made without a valid LUT may need IGST paid at the time of shipment.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('duty-drawback-explained', 'export', 'Duty drawback|refund of duty on exported goods',
  'What duty drawback is in India, how All Industry Rates and brand rates work, how to claim it on the shipping bill and when it is credited.',
  'Duty drawback gives back customs and some central duties that were paid on the inputs of an exported product. It is claimed through the shipping bill.',
  [('Two kinds of rate', ['All Industry Rate (AIR): a fixed percentage of the export value published by the government for each product. The exporter does not have to prove the inputs.', 'Brand rate: set for your own product when the AIR does not cover your actual duty. It needs an application with proof of inputs.']),
   ('How to claim', 'Declare the drawback in the shipping bill with the correct tariff item and the AIR serial number. After the export, customs processes the claim and credits it to your bank account.'),
   ('What can block it', 'A wrong tariff item, a missing bank account on file, a mismatch in the invoice value, or payment not realised in time.'),
   ('Drawback and other benefits', 'The rules do not allow you to claim the same duty twice. Duty drawback and RoDTEP can apply to the same shipment only if they cover different things. Check the notes of each scheme.')],
  [('How long does it take to get the drawback?', 'It is usually credited within weeks of a clean claim, but delays are common when data does not match.'),
   ('Is drawback taxable?', 'It is treated as export income for accounts. Check with your accountant.'),
   ('Where are the rates?', 'The government publishes the drawback schedule. Our export benefit finder shows what applies to your HS code.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('rodtep-explained', 'export', 'RoDTEP|what it is and how to claim it',
  'What RoDTEP is: Remission of Duties and Taxes on Exported Products, how it is credited, how rates are set and what exporters need to do.',
  'RoDTEP refunds taxes and duties that are embedded in an exported product but were not refunded by any other scheme, such as state levies and fuel taxes.',
  [('What it is', 'It replaced the older MEIS scheme in early 2021. Rates are a percentage of the export value, set by HS code, and are published in a schedule.'),
   ('How it is paid', 'The benefit is credited as an electronic scrip, which you can use to pay customs duty or transfer to another importer. It is claimed on the shipping bill.'),
   ('How to claim', ['Declare the RoDTEP claim and the right tariff item in the shipping bill.', 'Make sure your IEC and bank details are updated on ICEGATE.', 'Meet the conditions of the scheme, such as the minimum value and the exclusions.']),
   ('Exclusions and changes', 'Some products and some export routes are excluded, and rates change. Check the latest notification for your HS code before you price an order.')],
  [('Is RoDTEP the same as drawback?', 'No. Drawback returns central duties on inputs. RoDTEP returns embedded taxes that nothing else refunds.'),
   ('Can I use RoDTEP scrip?', 'Yes, to pay basic customs duty on imports, and it can be sold to others, as the scheme rules allow.'),
   ('Does it apply to services?', 'No. It is for goods.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('advance-authorisation-and-epcg', 'export', 'Advance Authorisation and EPCG|duty free imports for exporters',
  'How Advance Authorisation and EPCG work in India: duty free inputs and capital goods for exporters, export obligation and the common mistakes.',
  'Two schemes let an exporter import without paying customs duty, as long as the goods are used to make exports. Both come with an export obligation.',
  [('Advance Authorisation', 'You can import inputs that are physically used in the export product, without basic customs duty and often without IGST, for a fixed quantity and period. You then export the finished product and file proof with DGFT.'),
   ('EPCG', 'The Export Promotion Capital Goods scheme lets you import capital goods at a reduced or zero duty against an export obligation, usually a multiple of the duty saved, over a set number of years.'),
   ('Obligations', ['Export the committed value or quantity in the time allowed.', 'Keep records of inputs used and of the exports made.', 'File the redemption or discharge application on time.']),
   ('Risk', 'If the export obligation is missed, duty becomes payable with interest and a penalty can apply. Plan the capacity and the buyers before you take the licence.')],
  [('Who can apply?', 'Manufacturer exporters and merchant exporters tied to a supporting manufacturer, subject to the policy.'),
   ('Where do I apply?', 'On the DGFT portal, with your IEC.'),
   ('Can I sell the imported goods in India?', 'No. Under these schemes they are meant for export use. Selling them in India triggers the duty.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('rcmc-registration-export-promotion-council', 'export', 'RCMC|registration with an export promotion council',
  'What an RCMC is, which export promotion council to join, how to apply and why exporters need it for policy benefits.',
  'The Registration-cum-Membership Certificate (RCMC) proves you are a member of the export promotion council for your product. Many benefits under the foreign trade policy ask for it.',
  [('Why you need it', 'Exporters use the RCMC to claim policy benefits, to join trade fairs under council support, and to show buyers and banks that they are a registered exporter.'),
   ('Which council', 'Each product has a council or commodity board: for example engineering goods, textiles, pharmaceuticals, chemicals, leather, gems and jewellery, spices, marine products or agricultural goods. Your HS code tells you which one.'),
   ('How to apply', 'Apply to the council with your IEC, PAN, GST, bank details and product list. A fee and annual or lifetime membership applies, depending on the council.'),
   ('Keep it valid', 'RCMCs have a validity period. Renew on time or your benefit claims can be rejected.')],
  [('Is an RCMC needed for every exporter?', 'It is needed when you claim a benefit that asks for it. It is a good habit for all exporters.'),
   ('Can I have more than one RCMC?', 'Yes, if you export products that fall under different councils.'),
   ('Does the RCMC give money?', 'No. It is a membership certificate that lets you apply for benefits.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('certificate-of-origin-guide', 'export', 'Certificate of origin|preferential and non-preferential',
  'What a certificate of origin is, the difference between preferential and non-preferential, who issues it in India and how to apply.',
  'A certificate of origin states where the goods were made. The buyer needs it to clear customs, and sometimes to pay less duty.',
  [('Two kinds', [('table', ['Type', 'Purpose'], [['Non-preferential', 'Proves the origin of the goods for customs, a letter of credit or a trade rule. Does not give a lower duty.'], ['Preferential', 'Proves the goods qualify under a trade agreement and earn a lower or zero duty in the buyer country.']])]),
   ('Who issues it', 'Non-preferential certificates come from chambers of commerce and export promotion councils. Preferential certificates for each trade agreement are issued by the agencies that the agreement names, usually through an online system.'),
   ('What you need to apply', ['IEC, invoice, packing list, shipping bill details.', 'Proof that the goods meet the origin rule: for example a change of tariff heading or a minimum local value.', 'Cost sheet and supplier declarations when the rule needs them.']),
   ('Get it right', 'The product description, HS code and quantity must match the invoice. A mismatch is the most common reason a buyer customs office rejects the certificate.')],
  [('Do I need it for every export?', 'Only if the buyer, the bank or the trade agreement asks for it.'),
   ('How long is it valid?', 'Validity depends on the agreement and the issuing agency. Many are valid for a year from issue.'),
   ('Can I get it after shipment?', 'Some agreements allow late issue, but the rules differ. It is easier to apply before shipment.')],
  ('trade-agreement-preference-checker', 'Open the trade agreement checker'))
g('export-documents-checklist', 'export', 'Export documents checklist|what you need for every shipment',
  'The documents for exporting from India: commercial invoice, packing list, shipping bill, bill of lading, certificate of origin, insurance and more.',
  'Every export needs a core set of documents. Some products and some destinations add more.',
  [('Core documents', ['Commercial invoice with the Incoterm, the LUT number and the bank details.', 'Packing list with box counts, gross and net weight and dimensions.', 'Shipping bill from customs and the let export order.', 'Bill of lading or airway bill, and the shipping instructions.']),
   ('Depending on the case', ['Certificate of origin, preferential or non-preferential.', 'Phytosanitary, health, fumigation, free sale or quality certificates.', 'Insurance certificate if you sell on CIF or CIP.', 'Letter of credit and the bank forwarding documents.', 'Dangerous goods declaration and safety data sheet for hazardous cargo.']),
   ('After shipment', 'Bank realisation certificate (e-BRC), drawback and RoDTEP claim records, and the GST refund papers.'),
   ('Cross-check', 'The names, descriptions, weights and dates must agree across all the documents. Our document checker finds many of the common mismatches.')],
  [('Who prepares the commercial invoice?', 'The exporter. Use a clear description that matches the HS code.'),
   ('Do I need a packing list for a single box?', 'Yes. Customs and the carrier use it to verify the cargo.'),
   ('How many copies?', 'It depends on the buyer, bank and destination. Ask early, because lost originals delay payment.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('merchant-exporter-vs-manufacturer-exporter', 'export', 'Merchant exporter vs manufacturer exporter|what is the difference',
  'The difference between a merchant exporter and a manufacturer exporter in India, with GST, scheme and shipping bill implications.',
  'Both ship goods out of India, but the paperwork and the benefits are not the same.',
  [('Manufacturer exporter', 'Makes the goods and exports them. Can claim schemes such as Advance Authorisation and EPCG in its own name and shows itself as the manufacturer on the shipping bill.'),
   ('Merchant exporter', 'Buys from a supplier and exports. The shipping bill names both the merchant exporter and the supporting manufacturer. Some benefits are shared or need a declaration from the manufacturer.'),
   ('GST angle', 'A merchant exporter can buy from the supplier at the concessional 0.1 percent GST under the conditions in the rules, or buy at full rate and claim a refund. The supplier must meet the conditions, so check it before you rely on it.'),
   ('Which to choose', 'If you hold the product and can make it, own the manufacturing. If you source and sell, the merchant route is lighter but needs good supplier paperwork and cash control.')],
  [('Can I be both?', 'Yes. Many companies make some goods and trade in others.'),
   ('Who gets the drawback?', 'The claim is filed by the exporter on the shipping bill. The rules say who is entitled when two parties are named.'),
   ('Is a supporting manufacturer declaration needed?', 'For many schemes, yes.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
g('export-proceeds-realisation-fema-ebrc', 'export', 'Realising export proceeds|FEMA, FIRC and e-BRC',
  'How export payments are realised under FEMA, what e-BRC and FIRC are, and why the time limit and the bank records matter.',
  'An export is not complete until the money is in. The bank records and the time limit decide whether your GST refund and incentives go through.',
  [('FEMA rule', 'Exporters must bring the export proceeds into India within the period set by the RBI under FEMA. It has been nine months for most exports. Check the current master direction, because the limit can change.'),
   ('FIRC and e-BRC', ['The bank gives a foreign inward remittance certificate for each incoming payment.', 'The e-BRC is an electronic realisation certificate generated from the bank and shipping bill data. It is needed for GST refunds and some incentives.']),
   ('Common problems', ['Payment received from a third party or a different country than the buyer.', 'Wrong shipping bill number quoted to the bank.', 'Part payments, short payments or set offs that the bank does not link.']),
   ('If you cannot realise it', 'Talk to your bank before the limit ends. Extensions and write offs are possible but need documents and approval.')],
  [('Do I need an e-BRC for every shipment?', 'It is generated per shipping bill once the proceeds are linked.'),
   ('Can I accept payment from a third country?', 'It can be done, but the bank may ask for a reason. Plan it before the sale.'),
   ('What if the buyer does not pay?', 'Follow the bank and RBI rules for extension or write off, and consider credit insurance for the next time.')],
  ('export-benefit-finder', 'Open the export benefit finder'))
